<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models have become one of the central paradigms of generative modeling, offering state-of-the-art performance across a wide range of domains, including image synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, video generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, audio generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and text modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. The core idea is to define a forward noising process that gradually perturbs data into a tractable distribution, together with a reverse denoising process that reconstructs samples from noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, for applications in discrete and symbolic domains such as language, graphs, and molecules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">discrete-state diffusion models</span> have demonstrated advantages over their continuous counterparts, achieving strong perplexity and negative log-likelihood performance on language modeling benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. In the discrete setting, the forward noising process is typically formulated as a continuous-time Markov chain (CTMC) governed by a structured rate matrix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Two rate matrices are most commonly used: a <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform</span> rate matrix, whose stationary distribution is uniform over the state space, and an <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">absorbing</span> rate matrix, whose stationary distribution concentrates on a fully masked absorbing state. The absorbing-rate diffusion models, often referred to as masked diffusion models (MDMs), are particularly appealing due to its close connections to established architectures such as BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and conditional masked language models (CMLM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Empirically, MDMs have achieved competitive performance relative to autoregressive models on language modeling tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">
Table 1: </span>
<span id="S1.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Summary of results for absorbing-rate (masked) discrete diffusion samplers in terms of the number of steps needed to achieve <math id="S1.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>-accuracy in total-variation.</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="S1.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S1.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Estimation Error</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Num of steps</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reference</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">Score Entropy</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d\log M}{\varepsilon}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>ε</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d\log M}{\varepsilon}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Thm 2]</cite></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">Euler</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">Score Entropy</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d^{2}M^{2}}{\varepsilon^{3/4}}\right)^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d^{2}M^{2}}{\varepsilon^{3/4}}\right)^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Thm 4.1]</cite></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.4.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.4.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">DMPM</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">Score Entropy</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d}{\varepsilon^{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d}{\varepsilon^{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Thm 3.2.3]</cite></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.4.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.4.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Euler</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"><math id="S1.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">L</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"><math id="S1.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d}{\varepsilon^{1/2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#0000FF">𝒪</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">(</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">d</mi><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">ε</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d}{\varepsilon^{1/2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">[This paper, Thm <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>]</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.4.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.4.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">FHS</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Score Entropy</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Exactly <math id="S1.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S1.T1.4.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">[This paper, Thm <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>]</span></td></tr>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S1.T1.5" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_parbox ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:345.0pt;">
<p id="S1.T1.5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison:</span> Our result for the Euler method achieves the best dependence on <math id="S1.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> among <math id="S1.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping type algorithms while retaining small dependencies on <math id="S1.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and being <math id="S1.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>-free.
Moreover, we provide the first sampling guarantee for FHS, which attains the same sampling error as other methods but using <em id="S1.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exactly</em> <math id="S1.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> steps.</p>
<p id="S1.T1.5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.T1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notes:</span> <math id="S1.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> Only deterministic-step-size samplers are included in the table, because uniformization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> yields random (potentially infinite) converging steps.
<math id="S1.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> Here, <math id="S1.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the dimension (e.g., length of the generated sentence), <math id="S1.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the upper bound on the score estimates. The dependence on the vocabulary size <math id="S1.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is not explicitly shown.
<math id="S1.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> In <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we show that our <math id="S1.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> estimation error is more relaxed than score-entropy.
<math id="S1.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iv)</annotation></semantics></math> While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> does not show the dependence on <math id="S1.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in the main text, the dependence can be straightforwardly derived from their proof.</p>
</div></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these empirical advances, the theoretical understanding of discrete diffusion models, especially MDMs, remains limited.
Existing works using deterministic-step-size samplers have largely relied on convergence analyses in terms of Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence, most under the uniform-rate matrix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and, more recently, some under the absorbing-rate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
However, there are currently no lower bounds showing whether existing KL-based guarantees for discrete diffusion are tight. In <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">continuous</span> diffusion, prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> often finds that TV analyses yield sharper bounds and more accurate parameter dependence such as the dimension <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. This motivates a TV-based analysis for <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">discrete</span> diffusion to obtain tighter guarantees that potentially admit matching lower bounds. Doing so is nontrivial, since KL-specific tools such as change-of-measure arguments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> do not extend to TV, and the discontinuity of the underlying space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> introduces additional technical challenges.
Furthermore, these KL-based guarantees often require strong assumptions on the estimated score function. For example, all of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> require the assumption of a bounded score estimate, which needs typically be achieved through an additional clipping step during training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
These limitations give rise to our first central question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Question 1: Can we establish convergence guarantees with <em id="S1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">tighter</em> parameter dependencies via a direct analysis in TV distance? Moreover, can we derive matching lower bounds that complement the existing upper bounds?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, the First-Hitting Sampler (FHS) has been shown to be highly effective for generation with MDMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Unlike classical deterministic-step-size samplers, such as Euler methods, whose number of steps must grow unboundedly to achieve vanishing TV distance, FHS can produce a high-quality sample in exactly <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> steps, where <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the data dimension. Despite its empirical success, the theoretical error bound of FHS has not been explored. Given its different design philosophy, the analysis of FHS requires new techniques that explicitly exploit its underlying structure. This leads us to the next question below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Question 2. Given a <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-step sampling procedure like FHS, how to characterize the sampling error of the algorithm? Moreover, can we also derive a matching lower bound for FHS?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we address both questions and give affirmative answers to both of them.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Our Contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The central contributions of this work are (i) a new direct TV analysis tailored to the Euler method for masked diffusion models with a matching lower bound, and (ii) a novel analysis for the FHS algorithm, leading to
tight error bound that matches our lower bound.
Our detailed contributions include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improved Parameter Dependencies for the Euler Method (with straightforward generalization to <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping type samplers):</span> For the Euler method for the absorbing-rate (masked) discrete diffusion models,
we strengthen existing theoretical guarantees with an improved <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> dependence while retaining the best-known dependencies on <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the same guarantees can be straightforwardly extended to vanilla <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping samplers using the technique in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
In particular, we show that, in order to reach a slightly perturbed target distribution, the required number of steps is
<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(dS/\sqrt{\varepsilon}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(dS/\sqrt{\varepsilon}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Compared to prior results, ours yields an improvement by a factor of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, our analysis removes the need for bounded score assumptions and removes the need for a surrogate initialization typically required in previous works.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tight Lower Bound for the Euler Method:</span> We provide, to our knowledge, the first <em id="S1.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower-bound</em> analysis for absorbing-rate discrete diffusion models using the Euler method in TV distance. Combined with our improved upper-bound, we show that our bound is tight in both the data dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the target accuracy <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with constant step-sizes and without early-stopping.
Our result identifies the intrinsic limits of convergence when the Euler method is used.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Error Analysis for FHS and Tight Lower Bound:</span>
We perform a rigorous analysis for the First-Hitting Sampler (FHS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> for masked diffusion models, which generates a sample in exactly <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> steps.
Specifically, we show that FHS incurs no sampling error beyond that arising from score estimation itself. In particular, the resulting error bound is independent of both the data dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the vocabulary size <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
We further show that this bound is tight, by constructing a worst-case instance for which the FHS output error matches exactly the upper bound of the score estimation error. Our result further highlights the advantages of masked diffusion models, for which efficient finite-step sampling with dimension-free guarantees is possible.
Specifically, we develop a novel error analysis that decomposes the total sampling error into token-wise components. Our key insight is that FHS exploits an <span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intrinsic decoupling structure</span> in MDMs: the transition time <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and transition index <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> can be sampled exactly from the true reverse process, regardless of the accuracy of score estimation. Consequently, the overall sampling error depends solely on the estimation error incurred at each predicted token, and is therefore independent of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Related Works</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">See <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Full Related Works ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for a full list of related works. As follows we only include a brief survey of the most relevant works.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theory on Uniform-Rate Discrete Diffusion Models.</span> Considerable effort has been devoted to improving convergence guarantees for uniform-rate diffusion models. Unlike continuous diffusion, the resulting guarantees in the discrete setting crucially depend on the choice of sampler. Early analyses focused on uniformization-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, exact per-step samplers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, and <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping schemes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. More recently, theoretical guarantees have been established for DMPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> as well as for more practical Tweedie <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping and Euler method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
Notably, all of these results are derived in terms of KL divergence, which yields suboptimal guarantees when translated to total variation distance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theory on Masked (Absorbing-rate) Discrete Diffusion Models.</span>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> was the first to establish convergence guarantees for masked diffusion models under the <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping and the uniformization samplers, showing improved dimensional dependence compared to uniform-rate diffusion. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> studied faster convergence rates using the Euler method and a specialized uniformization scheme, the Mask-Aware Truncated Uniformization (MATU), which eliminates the need for a bounded score assumption. In parallel, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> derived improved dimension-dependent rates for the DMPM sampler, also without assuming bounded score.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison with concurrent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</span> After we submitted this paper to a conference, a concurrent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> was recently posted on arXiv, which also studies convergence guarantees for absorbing-rate discrete diffusion models albeit under a modified truncated <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping sampler (in addition to uniform-rate models, which is not the focus of our paper). The two papers differ in several important ways. (i) The two works make orthogonal improvements over prior convergence results for masked diffusion models. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> derives a convergence rate of <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\mathcal{D}/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\mathcal{D}/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> under the KL divergence, where <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\leq d\log S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\leq d\log S</annotation></semantics></math> is a distribution-dependent quantity, improving previous bounds in the dependence on <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and potentially <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, our analysis focuses on Euler discretization under the total variation (TV) metric for general distributions, improving prior results by a factor of <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ϵ</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the TV metric allows us to start from the all-mask singleton distribution (as is typical in practice) without resorting to a surrogate initialization. (ii) We establish the first lower bound for masked diffusion models, which matches our upper bound in its dependence on <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> does not provide a lower bound for masked diffusion models. (iii) We also analyze the recently introduced, highly efficient masked diffusion sampler FHS, and show that it attains provable <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-accuracy with the best-known sampling complexity of finite <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> steps for masked diffusion models. This sampler is not considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As in standard diffusion models, discrete diffusion models comprise a forward noising process and a reverse generation process. In this section, we describe two common frameworks used to model discrete diffusion dynamics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>CTMC Forward Process</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Discrete diffusion models can be modeled as continuous-time Markov chains (CTMCs) defined over the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">discrete</span> data space <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[S]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[S]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of tokens and each token is drawn from a vocabulary of size <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
In the forward process, the initial data is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in[S]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in[S]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, with corresponding probability mass function (p.m.f.) <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{S^{d}\times S^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{S^{d}\times S^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the rate matrix of the forward process. For states <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in[S]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in[S]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the instantaneous rate of transition from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The conditional distribution of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{t+\Delta t|t}(y|x)=\mathds{1}\left\{y=x\right\}+R_{t}(x,y)\Delta t+o(\Delta t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>o</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+\Delta t|t}(y|x)=\mathds{1}\left\{y=x\right\}+R_{t}(x,y)\Delta t+o(\Delta t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathds{1}\left\{\cdot\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}\left\{\cdot\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function. For a valid CTMC, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}(x,y)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}(x,y)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\neq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\neq y</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{y}R_{t}(x,y)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{y}R_{t}(x,y)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify computation for large <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, it is common to assume that tokens evolve <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">independently</span> and <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">homogeneously</span> across dimensions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Under this assumption, the forward conditional distribution factorizes as</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t|0}(x_{t}|x_{0})=\prod_{i=1}^{d}q_{t|0}^{i}(x_{t}^{i}|x_{0}^{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t|0}(x_{t}|x_{0})=\prod_{i=1}^{d}q_{t|0}^{i}(x_{t}^{i}|x_{0}^{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t|0}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t|0}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditional for the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th token. Equivalently, if we define the corresponding token-level rate matrix by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}^{tok}\in\mathbb{R}^{S\times S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}^{tok}\in\mathbb{R}^{S\times S}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> shows that</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}(x,y)=\begin{cases}R_{t}^{tok}(x^{i},y^{i}),&amp;\text{if }\mathrm{Ham}\left(x,y\right)=1,\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Ham</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}(x,y)=\begin{cases}R_{t}^{tok}(x^{i},y^{i}),&amp;\text{if }\mathrm{Ham}\left(x,y\right)=1,\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ham}\left(x,y\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ham}\left(x,y\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Hamming distance between <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}^{tok}=\beta_{t}R_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}^{tok}=\beta_{t}R_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a noise schedule <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we focus on the constant schedule <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>, as in prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the literature of CTMC framework,
the <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">absorbing-rate</span> (corresponding to masked diffusion model) matrix has attracted special attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Define one vocabulary word as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{[MASK]}\in[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{[MASK]}\in[S]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The rate matrix is given by</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{base}}:=\mathbf{1}_{S}e_{\mathrm{[MASK]}}^{\intercal}-I_{S}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{base}}:=\mathbf{1}_{S}e_{\mathrm{[MASK]}}^{\intercal}-I_{S},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a unit vector with only the <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th element being 1. Then, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}\approx\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}\approx\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a singleton at the all-mask state.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>CTMC Reverse Process and the Euler Method</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reverse process under the CTMC framework is defined as the exact time reversal of the forward CTMC, with initial distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{0}=q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:4.8pt;height:6.199999999999999pt;vertical-align:-1.9pt;" depth="1.9pt" height="4.3pt" width="4.8pt"><mi>q</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{0}=q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. In other words, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}=q_{T-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:4.8pt;height:6.199999999999999pt;vertical-align:-1.9pt;" depth="1.9pt" height="4.3pt" width="4.8pt"><mi>q</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}=q_{T-t}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Proposition 1]</cite>, the reverse process is also a CTMC with the rate matrix given by</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x,y)=R_{T-t}(y,x)\frac{q_{T-t}(y)}{q_{T-t}(x)},\quad x\neq y," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:7.7pt;height:6.8pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="6.8pt" width="7.7pt"><mi>R</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x,y)=R_{T-t}(y,x)\frac{q_{T-t}(y)}{q_{T-t}(x)},\quad x\neq y,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x,x)=-\sum_{y\neq x}\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:7.7pt;height:6.8pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="6.8pt" width="7.7pt"><mi>R</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:7.7pt;height:6.8pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="6.8pt" width="7.7pt"><mi>R</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x,x)=-\sum_{y\neq x}\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, to avoid instability near <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, one often employs <span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">early-stopping</span> by truncating the reverse chain at <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> for some small <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also, since the ratio <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{q_{t}(y)}{q_{t}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{q_{t}(y)}{q_{t}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> (a.k.a., the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">concrete score function</span>) is intractable, one typically train a neural-network to estimate it. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(y,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math> be such an estimator.
One typical loss is the score-entropy (SE) loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s;t)=\mathbb{E}_{x_{t}\sim q_{t}}\sum_{y:y\neq x_{t}}R_{t}(y,x_{t})\cdot\\
\left(s_{t}(y,x)-\frac{q_{t}(y)}{q_{t}(x)}-\frac{q_{t}(y)}{q_{t}(x)}\log\frac{s_{t}(y,x)}{q_{t}(y)/q_{t}(x)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s;t)=\mathbb{E}_{x_{t}\sim q_{t}}\sum_{y:y\neq x_{t}}R_{t}(y,x_{t})\cdot\\
\left(s_{t}(y,x)-\frac{q_{t}(y)}{q_{t}(x)}-\frac{q_{t}(y)}{q_{t}(x)}\log\frac{s_{t}(y,x)}{q_{t}(y)/q_{t}(x)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the marginal p.m.f. at time <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T-\delta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T-\delta]</annotation></semantics></math> in the sampling process. Since <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is unavailable, the sampling process starts with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the stationary distribution of the forward CTMC. Then, the continuous-path is discretized for practical algorithms. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{k}\}_{k\in[N]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{k}\}_{k\in[N]}</annotation></semantics></math> be the discretization points on which <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{T-t_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{T-t_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is accessible, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{N}=T-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{N}=T-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}:=t_{k+1}-t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}:=t_{k+1}-t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the step-sizes. Define the estimated reverse rate as</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{t_{k}}(x,y):=R_{T-t_{k}}(y,x)s_{T-t_{k}}(y,x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{t_{k}}(x,y):=R_{T-t_{k}}(y,x)s_{T-t_{k}}(y,x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">To achieve efficient sampling from the continuous-time process, one commonly used sampler is the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Euler method</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Our results for the Euler method in this paper naturally extend to the vanilla <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping and Tweedie <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping using the techniques in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span>
For each <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,\dots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,\dots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>, the next-token on the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th index is given as</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}_{t_{k+1}}=\begin{cases}a,&amp;\text{w.p.}\penalty\ \hat{R}_{k}^{i}(x_{t_{k}}^{i},a)(t_{k+1}-t_{k}),\penalty\ \forall a\neq x^{i}_{t_{k}}\\
x^{i}_{t_{k}},&amp;\text{w.p.}\penalty\ 1+\hat{R}_{k}^{i}(x_{t_{k}}^{i},x_{t_{k}}^{i})(t_{k+1}-t_{k})\end{cases}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>w.p.</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>w.p.</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}_{t_{k+1}}=\begin{cases}a,&amp;\text{w.p.}\penalty\ \hat{R}_{k}^{i}(x_{t_{k}}^{i},a)(t_{k+1}-t_{k}),\penalty\ \forall a\neq x^{i}_{t_{k}}\\
x^{i}_{t_{k}},&amp;\text{w.p.}\penalty\ 1+\hat{R}_{k}^{i}(x_{t_{k}}^{i},x_{t_{k}}^{i})(t_{k+1}-t_{k})\end{cases},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{R}_{k}^{i}(x_{t_{k}}^{i},a):=\hat{R}_{t_{k}}(x_{t_{k}},x_{t_{k}}^{-i}\oplus_{i}a)\penalty\ (\text{where}\penalty\ a\neq x_{t_{k}}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mo>⊕</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{k}^{i}(x_{t_{k}}^{i},a):=\hat{R}_{t_{k}}(x_{t_{k}},x_{t_{k}}^{-i}\oplus_{i}a)\penalty\ (\text{where}\penalty\ a\neq x_{t_{k}}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the token-wise rate, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x^{-i}\oplus_{i}a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{-i}\oplus_{i}a</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the vector obtained by replacing the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with token <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>D3PM and MDM</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Apart from the continuous-time Markov chain (CTMC) framework, discrete diffusion models can also be modeled as in Discrete Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (D3PMs).
In the <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">forward process</span>, still assuming that each dimension propagates independently as in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 CTMC Forward Process ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), the (token-wise) transition probability is represented as</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{i}_{t|0}(x_{t}^{i}|x_{0}^{i})=\mathrm{Cat}(\bar{Q}_{t}^{\intercal}e_{x_{0}^{i}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Cat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>⊺</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{i}_{t|0}(x_{t}^{i}|x_{0}^{i})=\mathrm{Cat}(\bar{Q}_{t}^{\intercal}e_{x_{0}^{i}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cat}(\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cat}(\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math> represents a categorical distribution with parameter <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{Q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{Q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the transition probability matrix.
Specifically, define the <span id="S2.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mask</span> transition matrix as</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{Q}_{t}=\alpha_{t}I+(1-\alpha_{t})\mathbf{1}_{S}e_{\mathrm{[MASK]}}^{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{Q}_{t}=\alpha_{t}I+(1-\alpha_{t})\mathbf{1}_{S}e_{\mathrm{[MASK]}}^{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is some pre-defined noise schedule, and in this paper we adopt a typical choice that <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=e^{-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=e^{-t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, a so-called “bridge distribution” <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{s|t,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{s|t,0}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;t</annotation></semantics></math> is particularly useful.
Indeed, under the mask transition in (<a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 2.3 D3PM and MDM ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), one can show that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{s}^{i}|x_{t}^{i},x_{0}^{i})=\begin{cases}\mathrm{Cat}(e_{x_{t}^{i}}),&amp;\text{if }x_{t}^{i}\neq\mathrm{[MASK]}\\
\mathrm{Cat}\Big(\frac{(1-\alpha_{s})e_{\mathrm{[MASK]}}+(\alpha_{s}-\alpha_{t})(e_{x_{0}^{i}})}{1-\alpha_{t}}\Big),&amp;\text{if }x_{t}^{i}=\mathrm{[MASK]}\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>Cat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>Cat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{s}^{i}|x_{t}^{i},x_{0}^{i})=\begin{cases}\mathrm{Cat}(e_{x_{t}^{i}}),&amp;\text{if }x_{t}^{i}\neq\mathrm{[MASK]}\\
\mathrm{Cat}\Big(\frac{(1-\alpha_{s})e_{\mathrm{[MASK]}}+(\alpha_{s}-\alpha_{t})(e_{x_{0}^{i}})}{1-\alpha_{t}}\Big),&amp;\text{if }x_{t}^{i}=\mathrm{[MASK]}\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, in the sampling process of MDMs with discretized time grid <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{k}\}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{k}\}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, a sample <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t_{k-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t_{k-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained token-wisely with the sampling probabilities <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{t_{k-1}}^{i}|x_{t_{k}})=\sum_{x_{0}^{i}}q(x_{t_{k-1}}^{i}|x_{t_{k}}^{i},x_{0}^{i})q(x_{0}^{i}|x_{t_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{t_{k-1}}^{i}|x_{t_{k}})=\sum_{x_{0}^{i}}q(x_{t_{k-1}}^{i}|x_{t_{k}}^{i},x_{0}^{i})q(x_{0}^{i}|x_{t_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
While in practice, in order to sample <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t_{k-1}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t_{k-1}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in MDMs, one typical way is to train a predictive model <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{i}_{\theta}(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{i}_{\theta}(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> to estimate the posterior conditional distribution <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{0}^{i}|x_{t_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{0}^{i}|x_{t_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, given the discretized time <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>’s and the current state <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the algorithm replace the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{x_{0}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{x_{0}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> above with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\hat{x}_{0}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\hat{x}_{0}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from the predictive model <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}^{i}(x_{t_{k}},t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}^{i}(x_{t_{k}},t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> at each step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One typical loss function for training <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">negative evidence lower bound</span> (NELBO) of the parameterized model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, given as</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})=\int_{0}^{1}\frac{\alpha_{t}^{\prime}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\;\mathbb{E}_{q_{t|0}(x_{t}\mid x_{0})}\left[\sum_{l:\,x_{t}^{l}=\mathrm{[MASK]}}e_{x_{0}^{l}}^{\top}\log\mu_{\theta}^{l}(x_{t},t)\right]dt," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mfrac><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})=\int_{0}^{1}\frac{\alpha_{t}^{\prime}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\;\mathbb{E}_{q_{t|0}(x_{t}\mid x_{0})}\left[\sum_{l:\,x_{t}^{l}=\mathrm{[MASK]}}e_{x_{0}^{l}}^{\top}\log\mu_{\theta}^{l}(x_{t},t)\right]dt,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}^{\prime}=\mathrm{d}\alpha_{t}/\mathrm{d}t=-e^{-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}^{\prime}=\mathrm{d}\alpha_{t}/\mathrm{d}t=-e^{-t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Interestingly, there is a close relationship <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> between the predictive model <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> in MDMs and the score estimator <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(y,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math> in the absorbing-rate CTMC:</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(\cdot,x)=\frac{\alpha_{t}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\,\mu_{\theta}(x,t)[\cdot]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(\cdot,x)=\frac{\alpha_{t}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\,\mu_{\theta}(x,t)[\cdot].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, with <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=e^{-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=e^{-t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
one can show that the forward process of such an MDM is equivalent to that of the time-homogeneous absorbing-rate CTMC. Therefore, such MDM shares the same reverse process with the absorbing-rate CTMC.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This relationship can also be generalized from between estimators into between true values, i.e., concrete score <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{q_{t}\!\left(x_{t}^{1},\cdots,\hat{x}_{t}^{i},\cdots,x_{t}^{d}\right)}{q_{t}\!\left(x_{t}^{1},\cdots,x_{t}^{i},\cdots,x_{t}^{d}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{q_{t}\!\left(x_{t}^{1},\cdots,\hat{x}_{t}^{i},\cdots,x_{t}^{d}\right)}{q_{t}\!\left(x_{t}^{1},\cdots,x_{t}^{i},\cdots,x_{t}^{d}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and true token-wise reverse conditional distribution <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}\!\left(\hat{x}_{t}^{i}|x_{t}^{\mathrm{UM}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>UM</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}\!\left(\hat{x}_{t}^{i}|x_{t}^{\mathrm{UM}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{q_{t}\!\left(x_{t}^{1},\cdots,\hat{x}_{t}^{i},\cdots,x_{t}^{d}\right)}{q_{t}\!\left(x_{t}^{1},\cdots,x_{t}^{i},\cdots,x_{t}^{d}\right)}=\frac{\alpha_{t}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\,q_{0}^{i}\!\left(\hat{x}_{t}^{i}|x_{t}^{UM}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{q_{t}\!\left(x_{t}^{1},\cdots,\hat{x}_{t}^{i},\cdots,x_{t}^{d}\right)}{q_{t}\!\left(x_{t}^{1},\cdots,x_{t}^{i},\cdots,x_{t}^{d}\right)}=\frac{\alpha_{t}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\,q_{0}^{i}\!\left(\hat{x}_{t}^{i}|x_{t}^{UM}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}^{UM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}^{UM}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the collection of unmasked coordinates. Given such a close relationship, we will use “MDMs” and “absorbing-rate diffusion models” interchangeably.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>First Hitting Sampler (FHS)</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First Hitting Sampler (FHS), as referenced in <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 2.4 First Hitting Sampler (FHS) ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, is a sampling algorithm specifically designed for MDMs, which was first proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
Instead of employing a pre-defined discretized time-grid as seen in previous samplers (e.g., <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping and the Euler’s method),
FHS simulates the sampling process in MDMs
by directly characterizing each individual unmasking event.
As a result, FHS avoids unnecessary intermediate steps and requires exactly
<math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> sampling steps to generate a complete sequence, making it a substantially more efficient sampler.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite such a significant empirical improvement, there still lacks theoretical characterization of the convergence error of FHS, i.e., how score estimation error influences the convergence rate of FHS. Furthermore, it also remains unclear whether the minimizer of the training objective (NELBO) for MDMs coincides with the minimizer of the score entropy loss. To address these open questions and provide a principled understanding of FHS, we will conduct analysis on FHS in <a href="#S4" title="4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>First Hitting Sampler (FHS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite></figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>
noise schedule <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=e^{-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=e^{-t}</annotation></semantics></math> with inverse <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}(u)=-\log u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}(u)=-\log u</annotation></semantics></math>,
pretrained masked diffusion model <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{d}\leftarrow[\mathrm{[MASK]},\mathrm{[MASK]},\ldots,\mathrm{[MASK]}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{d}\leftarrow[\mathrm{[MASK]},\mathrm{[MASK]},\ldots,\mathrm{[MASK]}]</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\tau_{d}}\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\tau_{d}}\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leftarrow d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leftarrow d</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algorithm1.4.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_upright">to</span> <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Sample <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n}\sim\mathrm{Uniform}([0,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Uniform</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n}\sim\mathrm{Uniform}([0,1])</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n-1}\leftarrow\alpha^{-1}\!\bigl(1-u_{n}^{1/n}(1-\alpha_{\tau_{n}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n-1}\leftarrow\alpha^{-1}\!\bigl(1-u_{n}^{1/n}(1-\alpha_{\tau_{n}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Randomly select <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly from <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\,i:\mathbf{x}_{n}^{(i)}=\mathrm{[MASK]}\,\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\,i:\mathbf{x}_{n}^{(i)}=\mathrm{[MASK]}\,\}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{n-1}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{n-1}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Sample <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\sim\mathrm{Cat}\!\bigl(\mu_{\theta}^{l}(\mathbf{x}_{n},\tau_{n-1})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.506em;" width="1.506em"><mi>Cat</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\sim\mathrm{Cat}\!\bigl(\mu_{\theta}^{l}(\mathbf{x}_{n},\tau_{n-1})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{n-1}^{(l)}\leftarrow z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{n-1}^{(l)}\leftarrow z</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.11.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span><math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Key Notations</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}\penalty\ (1\leq i\leq d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}\penalty\ (1\leq i\leq d)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th element of a vector <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[S]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[S]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{-i}\in[S]^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{-i}\in[S]^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th element removed.
Define <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ham}\left(x,y\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ham}\left(x,y\right)</annotation></semantics></math> as the Hamming distance between two vectors <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a positive integer <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]:=\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]:=\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{1}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{1}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> as a vector of length <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that contains all 1’s, and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> as an identity matrix of size <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our improved upper error bounds and the first convergence lower bound for the Euler method.
In particular, differently from most existing theoretical results (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>), our results are directly characterized by TV distance without providing an upper bound in KL divergence followed by invoking the Pinsker’s inequality. As a result, our approach yields tighter bounds on the number of steps and improves other parameter dependencies as well.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>General Decomposition in Total Variation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem shows the decomposition of the total-variation error incurred by a mismatched reverse process under general reverse rates. It will later be instantiated to obtain an improved parameter dependence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall that the reverse process <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> also follows a CTMC. Denote its initial distribution by <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the rate by <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{T-\delta},p_{T-\delta})\leq\mathrm{TV}(\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{0},p_{0})+\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\int_{t_{k}}^{t_{k+1}}\mathbb{E}_{x_{t}\sim\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}}\sum_{y:y\neq x_{t}}\left|\hat{R}_{t}(x_{t},y)-\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x_{t},y)\right|\mathrm{d}t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:4.8pt;height:6.199999999999999pt;vertical-align:-1.9pt;" depth="1.9pt" height="4.3pt" width="4.8pt"><mi>q</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>TV</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:4.8pt;height:6.199999999999999pt;vertical-align:-1.9pt;" depth="1.9pt" height="4.3pt" width="4.8pt"><mi>q</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.388em">∼</mo><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:1.7pt;height:2.2pt;vertical-align:-0.7pt;" depth="0.7pt" height="1.5pt" width="1.7pt"><mi mathsize="0.480em">q</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">−</mo><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:7.7pt;height:6.8pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="6.8pt" width="7.7pt"><mi>R</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{T-\delta},p_{T-\delta})\leq\mathrm{TV}(\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{0},p_{0})+\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\int_{t_{k}}^{t_{k+1}}\mathbb{E}_{x_{t}\sim\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}}\sum_{y:y\neq x_{t}}\left|\hat{R}_{t}(x_{t},y)-\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x_{t},y)\right|\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 General Decomposition in Total Variation ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that the final TV error between the marginal distributions of the true and mismatched processes can be upper-bounded by the sum of (1) the initial TV error and (2) the error accumulated along the sampling path from mismatched rate matrices.
Compared with prior KL-based analyses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, the TV decomposition yields an accumulation term characterized not by a Bregman divergence but by the absolute difference of the rate matrices. Such a dependence on the absolute rate difference was also seen in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The full proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 General Decomposition in Total Variation ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Proof of ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. Our argument starts by decomposing the TV distance into the initial error and the integral of its rate of change over time. While Reynolds’ Transport Theorem can be used to handle this rate of change in continuous spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Appendix K]</cite>, it cannot be directly applied in the discrete setting due to the absence of directional vectors. To address this, we develop a discrete analogue. We first prove a result showing that the rate of change in the marginal difference for the boundary terms vanishes (<a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ Appendix D Proof of ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), leaving only probability derivatives. Then, these are expressed through the Kolmogorov forward equations and rearranged, yielding a bound in terms of the absolute differences between the true and mismatched rate matrices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we characterize the convergence rate with explicit parameter dependencies for the Euler method. Our analysis is made only under the following estimation error.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The score estimation error satisfies that <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{TV}(s)\leq\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\text{score}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext>score</mtext></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{TV}(s)\leq\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\text{score}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{TV}(s):=\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(t_{k+1}-t_{k})\mathbb{E}_{x_{k}\sim q_{T-t_{k}}}\bigg[\sum_{y:y\neq x_{k}}R_{T-t_{k}}(y,x_{k})\left|s_{T-t_{k}}(y,x_{k})-\frac{q_{T-t_{k}}(y)}{q_{T-t_{k}}(x_{k})}\right|\bigg]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{TV}(s):=\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(t_{k+1}-t_{k})\mathbb{E}_{x_{k}\sim q_{T-t_{k}}}\bigg[\sum_{y:y\neq x_{k}}R_{T-t_{k}}(y,x_{k})\left|s_{T-t_{k}}(y,x_{k})-\frac{q_{T-t_{k}}(y)}{q_{T-t_{k}}(x_{k})}\right|\bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here we assume that the score estimation is accurate in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the expected sum of absolute differences between the rate matrices), a condition that naturally arises from our direct TV-based analysis. A similar criterion also appears in the TV-based analysis of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While both <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Theorem 1]</cite> capture the estimation error in terms of the sum of absolute rate differences, there is a key difference between the two. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Theorem 1]</cite> requires that the sum of difference is small for <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> time <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, which results in a loose bound around <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math> where the score function diverges for all <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. In comparison, <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> only requires the sum <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">average</span> over both <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to be small.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An alternative objective commonly used in the literature is the score entropy loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, which has also been adopted in prior theoretical analyses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
In <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we connect the two estimation errors and show that our <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is more relaxed than the score entropy error.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall the score entropy loss at time <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 CTMC Reverse Process and the Euler Method ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
If <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{t}(x,y)-\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x,y)=o(1),\penalty\ \forall t,x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:7.7pt;height:6.8pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="6.8pt" width="7.7pt"><mi>R</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{t}(x,y)-\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t}(x,y)=o(1),\penalty\ \forall t,x,y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{t\in(\delta,T)}\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s_{t};t)\lesssim\varepsilon_{\text{score}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">score</mtext><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{t\in(\delta,T)}\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s_{t};t)\lesssim\varepsilon_{\text{score}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, choosing <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\asymp\log(d/\sqrt{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\asymp\log(d/\sqrt{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\asymp\frac{\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\text{score}}^{\prime}}}{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≍</mo><mfrac><msqrt><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">score</mtext><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msqrt><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\asymp\frac{\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\text{score}}^{\prime}}}{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}=\kappa\min\left\{1,T-t_{k}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}=\kappa\min\left\{1,T-t_{k}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{TV}(s)\lesssim\sqrt{dS\cdot\varepsilon_{\text{score}}^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">score</mtext><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{TV}(s)\lesssim\sqrt{dS\cdot\varepsilon_{\text{score}}^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem provides our improved convergence rate for the absorbing-rate Euler method.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> hold. Also suppose that the number of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{[MASK]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{[MASK]}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in the data <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x_{0})\leq m_{0}=\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x_{0})\leq m_{0}=\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely. Using the absorbing-rate matrix in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.1 CTMC Forward Process ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), if we initialize with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝛅</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>, sample using the Euler method, and choose the step-sizes as <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}=\kappa\min\left\{1,T-t_{k}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}=\kappa\min\left\{1,T-t_{k}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(q_{0},p_{T-\delta})\lesssim de^{-T}+\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\text{score}}}+\kappa dS(1-e^{-T}+\log\delta^{-1})+d\delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">score</mtext></msub></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(q_{0},p_{T-\delta})\lesssim de^{-T}+\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\text{score}}}+\kappa dS(1-e^{-T}+\log\delta^{-1})+d\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus, if <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\asymp\sqrt{\varepsilon}/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≍</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\asymp\sqrt{\varepsilon}/d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\asymp\log(d/\sqrt{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\asymp\log(d/\sqrt{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\asymp\sqrt{\varepsilon}/(dS\log\delta^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≍</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\asymp\sqrt{\varepsilon}/(dS\log\delta^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, we achieve <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(q_{0},p_{T-\delta})\lesssim\sqrt{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(q_{0},p_{T-\delta})\lesssim\sqrt{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(dS/\sqrt{\varepsilon}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(dS/\sqrt{\varepsilon}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> sampling steps.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing to prior works, our study features the following improvements.</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Improving parameter dependence)</span> <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides the <span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">first</span> guarantee for the <span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">absorbing</span> discrete diffusion models to achieve <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> sampling complexity. This improves the dependence of the number of steps on <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> by a factor of <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>, while the dependencies on <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> remain to be as small as <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(dS)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(dS)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Exact initialization)</span> While all of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> require the algorithm to initialize from a surrogate distribution, here we can safely initialize from <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the singleton at all-mask state. The underlying reason is that while <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(q_{T}||\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}})=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(q_{T}||\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}})=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(q_{T},\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(q_{T},\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}})</annotation></semantics></math> does not diverge due to irregularities of singletons. Thus, building upon <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 General Decomposition in Total Variation ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the convergence error can still be well-controlled even if starting from <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=\bm{\delta}_{\mathrm{[MASK]}^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Removing boundedness assumption)</span> Differently from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, we do not require that the score estimates are bounded. Such an assumption can be removed thanks to our direct TV-based analysis. In prior KL-based analyses, the score entropy term involves a logarithmic factor that becomes unstable when the estimated score is too small or too large <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. In contrast, our TV-based analysis only depends on the absolute difference induced by score estimation, avoiding this instability and removing the need for a bounded score estimate.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we study a special case for which a <span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-early-stopped</span> result can be obtained.
In particular, we introduce an extra parameter <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(q_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(q_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> that characterizes the likelihood of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{[MASK]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{[MASK]}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which first appeared in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
Note that in practice, <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(q_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(q_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is typically very small, reflecting the fact that only a small fraction of tokens are masked in the data (aside from a few corrupted entries).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma(q_{0}):=\min_{i\in[d]}\frac{q_{0}^{i}(\mathrm{[MASK]}|x^{-i})}{\max_{a^{i}\in[S]:a^{i}\neq\mathrm{[MASK]}}q_{0}^{i}(a^{i}|x^{-i})}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(q_{0}):=\min_{i\in[d]}\frac{q_{0}^{i}(\mathrm{[MASK]}|x^{-i})}{\max_{a^{i}\in[S]:a^{i}\neq\mathrm{[MASK]}}q_{0}^{i}(a^{i}|x^{-i})}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, under the same set of assumptions in <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and choosing constant step-sizes <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}\equiv\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}\equiv\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(q_{0},p_{T})\lesssim de^{-T}+\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\text{score}}}+\kappa dS(1-e^{-T}+\gamma(q_{0})^{-1/2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">score</mtext></msub></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>κ</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(q_{0},p_{T})\lesssim de^{-T}+\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\text{score}}}+\kappa dS(1-e^{-T}+\gamma(q_{0})^{-1/2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus, if we let <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\asymp\log(d/\sqrt{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\asymp\log(d/\sqrt{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\asymp\sqrt{\varepsilon}/(dS)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≍</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\asymp\sqrt{\varepsilon}/(dS)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(q_{0},p_{T})\lesssim\sqrt{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(q_{0},p_{T})\lesssim\sqrt{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(dS/\sqrt{\varepsilon}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(dS/\sqrt{\varepsilon}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> sampling steps.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Convergence Lower Bound for the Euler Method</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the strong performance guarantees of the Euler method, an important question is whether these upper bounds are tight, as this directly implies the fundamental theoretical limits of the algorithm. As follows, we provide a convergence lower bound under the absorbing-rate using the Euler sampler, assuming no estimation error.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that the Euler method with constant step-sizes is used such that <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}=\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}=\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then,
there exists <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(q^{*})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(q^{*})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that in order to achieve <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(q^{*},p_{T})\lesssim\sqrt{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(q^{*},p_{T})\lesssim\sqrt{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of steps must satisfy <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\tilde{\Omega}(d/\sqrt{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\tilde{\Omega}(d/\sqrt{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 3.3 Convergence Lower Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is the <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">first</span> convergence lower bound for the Euler method, and in the discrete diffusion literature at large. It shows that in order to achieve <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>-TV error, the number of steps required for the Euler method is at least in the order of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/\sqrt{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ε</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/\sqrt{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> (ignoring log-factors). If combined with <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it shows that our upper bound on the number of steps is tight in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (up to log-factors).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The full proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 3.3 Convergence Lower Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is provided in <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Proof of ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.
The key idea is to couple the forward process starting from a singleton <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}_{\bm{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>𝒂</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}_{\bm{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> with another forward process that starts from <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\gamma}=:q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\gamma}=:q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a construction
enables an explicit characterization of the score function and, consequently, the Euler updates at each step. Because tokens remain fixed once they are unmasked, we can derive the final element-wise mask probability <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, which captures the key dynamics of the process. Comparing <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> with the resulting distribution then yields a lower bound on the TV distance, from which we obtain the required relationship of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the step complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide our convergence analysis of FHS (see <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 2.4 First Hitting Sampler (FHS) ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Our analysis will be performed under the following assumption on the score estimation error.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{SE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{SE}</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 CTMC Reverse Process and the Euler Method ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Suppose that <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int_{0}^{+\infty}\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s;t)\mathrm{d}t\leq\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}_{\mathrm{score}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>score</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int_{0}^{+\infty}\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s;t)\mathrm{d}t\leq\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}_{\mathrm{score}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the studies using the integrated score entropy loss, the time integral is typically truncated at <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\asymp\log(d/\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\asymp\log(d/\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. We instead extend the upper limit to <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, for two reasons. First, for such a choice of <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, taking <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(y,x)\asymp\frac{e^{-t}}{1-e^{-t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≍</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(y,x)\asymp\frac{e^{-t}}{1-e^{-t}}</annotation></semantics></math> yields
(cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>): <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{T}^{+\infty}\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s;t)\mathrm{d}t\lesssim\int_{T}^{+\infty}\frac{1}{e^{t}-1}\mathrm{d}t\lesssim\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≲</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{T}^{+\infty}\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s;t)\mathrm{d}t\lesssim\int_{T}^{+\infty}\frac{1}{e^{t}-1}\mathrm{d}t\lesssim\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, extending the integration range to <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> is asymptotically equivalent, as the additional tail contributes at most <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, we show in the following proposition that such integrated score entropy loss is equivalent to the standard training protocol (i.e., NELBO) in (<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.3 D3PM and MDM ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) for masked diffusion models up to a fixed constant that depends only on the data distribution <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose there are no <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{[MASK]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{[MASK]}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in the data. Then we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\int_{0}^{+\infty}\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s;t)\,\mathrm{d}t=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}[\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})]-\sum_{k=1}^{d}\frac{1}{k}\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}_{k}}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{M}_{k}}H\left(q_{0}^{l}(\cdot|x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{+\infty}\mathcal{L}_{SE}(s;t)\,\mathrm{d}t=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}[\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})]-\sum_{k=1}^{d}\frac{1}{k}\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}_{k}}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{M}_{k}}H\left(q_{0}^{l}(\cdot|x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{k}\subseteq[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{k}\subseteq[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{M}_{k}|=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{M}_{k}|=k</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\ldots,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\ldots,d</annotation></semantics></math>, denotes the subset with exactly <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> token indices uniformly sampled from the total index set, <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}=[d]\setminus\mathcal{M}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}=[d]\setminus\mathcal{M}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the entropy, <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}}:=\{x_{0}^{l}\}_{l\in\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}}:=\{x_{0}^{l}\}_{l\in\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{0}^{l}(\cdot|x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}^{l}(\cdot|x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the conditional distribution of <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{SE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{SE}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 CTMC Reverse Process and the Euler Method ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), and <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is the NELBO defined in (<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.3 D3PM and MDM ‣ 2 Preliminaries of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). In other words, the expected NELBO <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}[\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}[\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})]</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent (up to a constant) to the integrated <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{SE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{SE}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> establishes the effectiveness of the training process for MDMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, which aims to minimize <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}[\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}[\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, our proof shows that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}[\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})]-\sum_{k=1}^{d}\frac{1}{k}\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}_{k}}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{M}_{k}}H\left(q_{0}^{l}(\cdot|x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}})\right)\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}[\mathcal{L}_{\infty}(x_{0})]-\sum_{k=1}^{d}\frac{1}{k}\mathbb{E}_{x_{0}\sim q_{0}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}_{k}}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{M}_{k}}H\left(q_{0}^{l}(\cdot|x_{0}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}^{c}})\right)\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and the equality holds if and only if <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{0}^{l}(\cdot|x_{t}^{UM})=\mu_{\theta}^{l}(x_{t},t)[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}^{l}(\cdot|x_{t}^{UM})=\mu_{\theta}^{l}(x_{t},t)[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}^{UM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}^{UM}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the collection of unmasked coordinates of <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that the right-most summation term
is an attainable lower bound in the training process,
which can be reached when the trained model is perfect.
The full proof of <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is in <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Proof of ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now provide the error bound for FHS in the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose there are no <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{[MASK]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{[MASK]}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in the data, and suppose that <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> holds.
Then,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textstyle\mathrm{KL}(q_{0}||\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{FHS}})\leq\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}_{\mathrm{score}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>FHS</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>score</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\mathrm{KL}(q_{0}||\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{FHS}})\leq\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}_{\mathrm{score}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{FHS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>FHS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{FHS}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the output distribution induced by the FHS algorithm.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that FHS can achieve <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-KL error in exactly <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> sampling steps. This is the <span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">first</span> result that shows that discrete diffusion samplers could achieve vanishing convergence error (as the training error decreases) within the <span id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">finite</span> number of steps.
Notably, the resulting error bound introduces no additional sources of error beyond score estimation error and exhibits no further dependence on either the data dimension <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> or the vocabulary size <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. This highlights the efficiency of the FHS algorithm compared to previous diffusion samplers, which generally require substantially more than <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> steps to reach the same level of accuracy. More generally, our result is a further showcase of the advantage of choosing masked diffusion models over their uniform-rate counterpart.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The full proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is provided in <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Proof of ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>. We highlight our main idea as follows. We start by showing in <a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3. ‣ Appendix I FHS Sampling in CTMC Framework: and its Proof ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> the decoupling property of MDMs: the transition time <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and transition index <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> can be sampled exactly from true reverse process, and remain independent of the estimation error introduced by <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then use this decoupling property to decompose the path-wise error into the error arising from predicting <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> in each sampling step. Finally, we transform the path-wise error to resulting sampling error on <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> using data processing inequality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Continuing the error upper bound of FHS in <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we now investigate whether the upper bound is tight. As follows, we derive a lower bound in <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Specifically, since <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> allows any form of <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> resulting from the training procedure, our chosen <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, as one particular instance, provides an error lower bound over the class of all such estimators.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a pair <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{0},\mu_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{0},\mu_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(q_{0}||\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{FHS}})\geq\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}_{\mathrm{score}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>FHS</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>score</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(q_{0}||\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{FHS}})\geq\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}_{\mathrm{score}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is the <span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">first</span> convergence lower bound for the FHS algorithm. Specifically, it shows that the upper bound in <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is exactly tight.
The full proof is provided in <a href="#A11" title="Appendix K Proof of ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K</span></a>. Different from the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 3.3 Convergence Lower Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we construct both a target distribution <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and a special form of the estimated predictive model</span> <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, such a construction corresponds to a worst-case pair <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{0},\mu_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{0},\mu_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can possibly be obtained during the training under <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Convergence Guarantees for the FHS Algorithm ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Such a construction allows us to explicitly characterize both the score estimation error and the final KL convergence error.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we presented a novel direct TV analysis for discrete diffusion models that yields tighter parameter dependencies than prior KL-based results.
Our framework improves convergence rates, removes the need for bounded score assumptions and surrogate initialization, and establishes the first tight lower bounds in TV.
Further,
we showed that FHS incurs no sampling error beyond that arising from score estimation itself, without additional dependence on the system parameters. We then showed that this error bound is tight via a lower bound by a worst-case construction.
Looking ahead, an interesting direction is to
investigate high-order variants of FHS for further acceleration.</p>
</div>
</section>
