<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Full Related Works</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Studies on Discrete Diffusion Models.</span> Due to the large portion of works, we will only include those early works and those most relevant works recently. Compared to continuous-space diffusion models, discrete-space diffusion models are emerging as strong alternatives for generative tasks involving symbolic data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> (see also surveys: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>). The continuous-time formulation of discrete diffusion was first introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> proposed the score-entropy estimation loss and demonstrated empirical success in text generation. They further introduced Tweedie <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping, a new sampler derived from an approximation of Tweedie’s formula. For per-step updates, most empirical implementations adopt categorical sampling, which has shown strong practical performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, an increasing number of empirical studies have focused on masked diffusion models, which is often found to deliver superior performance in text and image applications. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> simplified the variational training objective into a weighted cross-entropy integral and introduced a state-dependent masking schedule to dynamically adjust rates for better generation quality. Building on this, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> reparameterized the concrete score as a time-dependent scalar and a conditional distribution, leading to the Reparameterized Absorbing Discrete Diffusion (RADD) model for efficient training and sampling. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> exploited the absorbing state structure to derive a tighter ELBO through Rao-Blackwellization and proposed a semi-autoregressive decoding strategy for flexible sequence generation. Building on this line of work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> introduced the First-Hitting Sampler, which exactly samples from the continuous-time diffusion process under the assumption of a perfectly learned score. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> developed a pre-trained model for masked diffusion models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theory on Uniform-Rate Discrete Diffusion Models.</span> In contrast to the extensive theoretical literature on continuous diffusion models, rigorous results for discrete diffusion remain relatively limited. An early contribution is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, which analyzed <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping under the total variation metric but relied on strong estimation assumptions and exhibited unfavorable parameter dependencies. Subsequent works shifted focus to controlling the score–entropy estimation error. In particular, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> established convergence guarantees for the uniformization sampler on the <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional hypercube, later extended to general discrete spaces <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[S]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[S]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
For deterministic step-size methods, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> assumed access to an exact per-step solver, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> analyzed the more practical <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping sampler. Among these works, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> required control of the score–entropy loss along the entire continuous sampling trajectory, whereas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> imposed this requirement only on the discrete time grid. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> developed an alternative analysis that avoids the Girsanov change-of-measure technique, yielding improved parameter dependencies and directly motivating the present work.
Notably, all of these analyses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> are conducted in terms of KL divergence, which leads to suboptimal bounds when converted to total variation distance. Beyond convergence analysis, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> proposed a theoretically motivated improvement to discrete classifier-free guidance (CFG), and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> extended discrete diffusion techniques to continuous data distributions via quantization.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theory on Masked (Absorbing-rate) Discrete Diffusion Models.</span>
On the theoretical side, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> was the first to establish convergence guarantees for masked diffusion models, showing improved dimensional dependence compared to uniform-rate diffusion. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> studied faster convergence rates using the Euler method and a specialized uniformization scheme, the Mask-Aware Truncated Uniformization (MATU), which eliminates the need for a bounded score assumption. In parallel, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> derived improved dimension-dependent rates for the DMPM sampler, also without the need of a bounded score. From a different perspective, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> provided theoretical guarantees for masked language models. Beyond the analysis of unconditional models, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> examined classifier-free guidance in the context of masked diffusion models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison with concurrent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</span> After we submitted this paper to a conference, a concurrent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> was recently posted on arXiv, which also studies convergence guarantees for absorbing-rate discrete diffusion models albeit under a modified truncated <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping sampler (in addition to uniform-rate models, which is not the focus of our paper). The two papers differ in several important ways. (i) The two works make orthogonal improvements over prior convergence results for masked diffusion models. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> derives a convergence rate of <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\mathcal{D}/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\mathcal{D}/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> under the KL divergence, where <math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\leq d\log S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\leq d\log S</annotation></semantics></math> is a distribution-dependent quantity, improving previous bounds in the dependence on <math id="A1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and potentially <math id="A1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, our analysis focuses on Euler discretization under the total variation (TV) metric for general distributions, improving prior results by a factor of <math id="A1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>ϵ</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the TV metric allows us to start from the all-mask singleton distribution (as is typical in practice) without resorting to a surrogate initialization. (ii) We establish the first lower bound for masked diffusion models, which matches our upper bound in its dependence on <math id="A1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> does not provide a lower bound for masked diffusion models. (iii) We also analyze the recently introduced, highly efficient masked diffusion sampler FHS, and show that it attains provable <math id="A1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-accuracy with the best-known sampling complexity of finite <math id="A1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> steps for masked diffusion models. This sampler is not considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>List of Notations</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathds{1}\left\{x=y\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}\left\{x=y\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> which equals 1 only if <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=y</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,d</annotation></semantics></math>,
we write <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector where only the <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th element is 1 and other elements are <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>’s, and we write <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the distribution of a singleton whose p.m.f. is <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a positive integer <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[S]:=\{1,\dots,S\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[S]:=\{1,\dots,S\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="A2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{1}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{1}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> as a vector of length <math id="A2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that contains all 1’s, and <math id="A2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> as an identity matrix of size <math id="A2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\times S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="A2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the number of <math id="A2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{[MASK]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{[MASK]}</annotation></semantics></math> states in the vector <math id="A2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Proof of <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 3.2 Convergence Upper Bound for the Euler Method ‣ 3 Convergence Guarantees for the Euler Method ‣ Sharp Convergence Rates for Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{[MASK]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>MASK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{[MASK]}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in the vector <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. From the definition, the TV loss at time <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies that</p>
<table id="A3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{TV}^{2}(s;T-t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{TV}^{2}(s;T-t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\mathbb{E}_{x_{k}\sim\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t_{k}}}\left[\sum_{y:y\neq x_{k}}\left|\hat{R}_{t_{k}}(x_{k},y)-\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t_{k}}(x_{k},y)\right|\right]\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.388em">∼</mo><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:1.7pt;height:2.2pt;vertical-align:-0.7pt;" depth="0.7pt" height="1.5pt" width="1.7pt"><mi mathsize="0.480em">q</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">−</mo><msub><mpadded style="width:6.5pt;height:7.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="7.1pt" width="6.5pt"><mover accent="true"><mpadded style="width:7.7pt;height:6.8pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;" depth="0.0pt" height="6.8pt" width="7.7pt"><mi>R</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mpadded><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\mathbb{E}_{x_{k}\sim\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle q\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle q\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t_{k}}}\left[\sum_{y:y\neq x_{k}}\left|\hat{R}_{t_{k}}(x_{k},y)-\mathchoice{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\displaystyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\displaystyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 3.0mu\reflectbox{$\textstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-3.0mu\textstyle R\mkern 3.0mu$}}$}\mkern-3.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}{\mkern 2.0mu\reflectbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\vec{\reflectbox{$\mkern-2.0mu\scriptscriptstyle R\mkern 2.0mu$}}$}\mkern-2.0mu}_{t_{k}}(x_{k},y)\right|\right]\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
